CloudBurrow patch 17 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1057, #1103).

The bug: each DROP of a table, view or function rebuilds the root catalog
with every builtin function (resetCatalog), and the catalog it replaces is
freed in the engine's WebAssembly memory only by its finalizer, after a Go
collection. The emulator's Go heap is mostly that WebAssembly memory, one
large []byte, so a collection waits for the heap to double past it; the
retired catalogs piled up between collections and the memory grew at
every DROP. Measured natively on an Apple silicon Mac, with patches 0001
to 0016: 120 CREATE TABLE + DROP TABLE pairs in one dataset took the
emulator from 237 to 603 MiB RSS (6 collections in all).

The patch collects (runtime.GC) at the start of each rebuild, so the
finalizers of the catalogs earlier rebuilds retired free them and their
memory is reused. The same 120 pairs: 169 to 171 MiB, flat from the 20th;
a pair took 33-40 ms after the first 20 (66 ms each over those), against
30-34 ms before.

